# Server-sent events

Sometimes the server has news and the browser only needs to listen: a
notification, an order changing state, a build log, the progress of a long
job. Server-sent events are made for that. The browser opens an ordinary
`GET` request, and the server keeps the response open and writes events to
it as things happen.

Compared with a [WebSocket](/docs/controllers/websockets), an event stream
only goes one way, from server to browser. In return:

- It is plain HTTP, so proxies, load balancers and HTTP/2 handle it like
  any other response, and the browser's usual cookie and CORS rules apply.
- The browser's `EventSource` reconnects by itself if the connection drops,
  and tells the server the last event it saw, so it can pick up where it
  left off.

When the browser needs to talk back, it sends ordinary requests. If it
talks back a lot, use a WebSocket instead.

In Howdy an event stream is an ordinary route. It goes through the same
middleware, [guards](/docs/controllers/guards) and versioning as any other,
and the handler finishes with `sse.start` where another would return a page.

## A first stream

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/sse

pub fn orders_controller() {
  controller.new("orders")
  |> controller.get("/events", fn(ctx: Context) {
    sse.new(fn(stream) {
      sse.join(stream, "orders")
      Nil
    })
    |> sse.start(ctx)
  })
}
```

Anything in your program can now send an event to every browser listening,
from a handler, a service or a background job:

```gleam
import gleam/json

sse.broadcast(
  "orders",
  sse.json(json.object([#("id", json.int(42))])) |> sse.name("order.created"),
)
```

In the browser:

```js
const events = new EventSource("/orders/events")

events.addEventListener("order.created", (event) => {
  const order = JSON.parse(event.data)
  console.log("new order", order.id)
})
```

There are three parts to every stream, as with a WebSocket:

- `sse.new` takes the function to run when the stream opens. What it
  returns is the stream's starting state, here `Nil`.
- Callbacks such as `on_message` handle what happens next. Each is given the
  stream to send on and the current state.
- Each callback returns what to do now: `sse.continue(state)` to carry on,
  or `sse.stop()` to end the stream.

Each stream runs in its own process, so its state is private to it, and a
crash in one stream does not touch any other.

## Events

An event is some data with up to three optional fields. Build one with
`sse.event` for text or `sse.json` for JSON, then add the others:

```gleam
sse.event("Deploy finished")
|> sse.name("deploy")
|> sse.id("1042")
|> sse.retry(5000)
```

- `name` is the event's type. The browser delivers named events to
  `addEventListener(name, ...)`, and unnamed ones to `onmessage`.
- `id` is what the browser sends back when it reconnects. See
  [Picking up where you left off](#Picking-up-where-you-left-off).
- `retry` tells the browser how many milliseconds to wait before
  reconnecting.

Data spanning several lines arrives in the browser as it was sent. An event
is an ordinary record, `sse.Event`, so tests can compare events with `==`.

Send to the stream's own client with `sse.send`:

```gleam
let _ = sse.send(stream, sse.event("hello"))
```

It returns a `Result`, which is an error when the client has gone away.
There is rarely anything useful to do about that, since the stream is about
to end and `on_close` will run.

## Topics

A stream joins a topic with `sse.join`, and `sse.broadcast` sends an event
to every stream on a topic. They work like WebSocket
[channels](/docs/controllers/websockets#Channels):

- A topic is just a string, and exists while it has members.
- A stream that ends leaves all its topics by itself. Call `sse.leave` to
  stop listening while staying open.
- `broadcast` returns at once. Each stream sends from its own process, so a
  slow client never holds up the others, or the caller.
- `sse.size(topic)` tells you how many streams are on a topic.
- If your app runs on several connected Erlang nodes, a broadcast reaches
  the streams on all of them.

Event stream topics are separate from WebSocket channel topics, even when
the names match, because the two carry different kinds of message. A page
that has both to serve broadcasts to each.

## Messages from your own code

To send a value to the stream's own code rather than straight to the
client, use `sse.subject`. Whatever is sent to it arrives at `on_message`,
which decides what to send and can update the state:

```gleam
pub type Progress {
  Step(done: Int, of: Int)
  Finished
}

fn job_progress(ctx: Context) {
  use id <- param.string(ctx, "id")

  sse.new(fn(stream) {
    progress.watch(id, sse.subject(stream))
    Nil
  })
  |> sse.on_message(fn(stream, state, message) {
    case message {
      Step(done:, of:) -> {
        let _ =
          sse.send(
            stream,
            sse.json(json.object([#("done", json.int(done)), #("of", json.int(of))]))
              |> sse.name("progress"),
          )
        sse.continue(state)
      }
      Finished -> {
        let _ = sse.send(stream, sse.event("") |> sse.name("finished"))
        sse.stop()
      }
    }
  })
  |> sse.start(ctx)
}
```

Here `progress.watch` stands for your own code, which keeps the subject and
later calls `process.send(subject, Step(3, 10))`.

`presence.subscribe` from `howdy/websocket/presence` sends to a subject in
the same way, so a stream can show who is online. Callbacks run in the
stream's own process, so a stream can be tracked with
`presence.track(people(), process.self(), ...)` like a socket.

## Ending a stream

`sse.stop()` ends the stream, and `on_close` runs once when a stream ends,
however that happens: your callback stopped it, the client went away, or
the server is shutting down. Use it to tidy up. Nothing can be sent from
there.

The browser's `EventSource` reconnects after a stream ends, which is what
you want when a connection drops, but not when the stream has nothing more
to say. Either close it from the browser with `events.close()` when it
receives a final event, as the `finished` event above allows, or answer the
reconnecting request with a status other than `200`, such as `204`, which
tells `EventSource` to stop.

When the server stops, open streams end straight away, so browsers
reconnect, perhaps to another server, rather than waiting on one that is
going away.

## Picking up where you left off

When a browser reconnects it sends the `id` of the last event it saw, and
`sse.last_event_id(stream)` returns it. A stream whose events carry IDs can
send what was missed before anything new:

```gleam
sse.new(fn(stream) {
  sse.join(stream, "orders")
  case sse.last_event_id(stream) {
    Some(id) ->
      list.each(orders.changed_since(id), fn(order) {
        let _ = sse.send(stream, order_event(order))
      })
    None -> Nil
  }
  Nil
})
```

Join the topic first, as here, so nothing broadcast while catching up is
lost. The client may then see an event twice, so make events safe to apply
more than once, for instance by carrying the whole new state of an order
rather than a change to it.

Howdy does not store events for you; what "since" means is up to your app,
whether that is a sequence number, a timestamp or a database row.

## Keeping streams open

Proxies and load balancers close connections that have been quiet for a
while. To stop that, every stream sends a comment every 15 seconds, which
browsers ignore. Change the interval with `sse.heartbeat(milliseconds)`, or
turn it off with `sse.no_heartbeat`.

The heartbeat also notices clients that went away without a word, such as a
laptop closed mid-stream: when one cannot be sent, the stream ends and
`on_close` runs.

Responses carry `x-accel-buffering: no`, which asks nginx and proxies like
it to pass events on as they come rather than buffering them.

## Who may connect

An event stream is an ordinary `GET`, so the browser's usual rules apply.
Another website cannot read your stream unless your
[CORS](/docs/middleware/cors) settings allow it, and `EventSource` sends
cookies to your own site, so a guard that reads the session cookie protects
a stream like any other route. `EventSource` cannot set headers, so a guard
that expects an `authorization` header will not work from a browser.

## Testing streams

`howdy/testing` runs a stream without a socket. `testing.open_events` opens
the stream a response describes, `testing.next_event` waits for the next
event it sends, and `testing.close_events` ends it as a client going away
would:

```gleam
import howdy/sse
import howdy/testing

pub fn new_orders_are_announced_test() {
  let stream =
    testing.get("/orders/events")
    |> testing.send(app())
    |> testing.open_events

  sse.broadcast("orders", sse.event("42") |> sse.name("order.created"))

  assert testing.next_event(stream)
    == Ok(sse.event("42") |> sse.name("order.created"))
  testing.close_events(stream)
}
```

The stream's `on_open` has run by the time `open_events` returns, so it is
already on its topics. Set the `last-event-id` header on the request to test
a reconnect. `testing.sent_events` returns everything sent so far without
waiting, which suits events sent from `on_open`. Heartbeats are not sent in
tests.

A guard that turns the request away answers as usual, so check the status
before opening the stream.
